<worksheet>
<title>Applications of Quadratic Equations</title>
<page>
<objectives>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this section we will see how to:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Solve a general quadratic application.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Solve a projectile motion application.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Solve a basic economics problem.
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Solve a geometry application problem.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</objectives>
<p>
    When solving a quadratic application problem, we may be trying to determine either the <m>y</m>-intercept, the <m>x</m>-intercepts or the vertex of a quadratic equation.
    If we are trying to determine the <m>y</m>-intercept, we are looking for terms like starting point or initial value since the <m>y</m>-intercept occurs when <m>x=0</m>. 
    Some key terms we might see for the <m>x</m>-intercepts are questions asking for an amount (of <m>x</m>, "how many...?") or a time (<q>when does...?</q>). If a 
    question is looking for the vertex, which is the highest or lowest point on a quadratic equation, it will ask about a maximum (highest) or minimum (lowest).
</p>
<example>
    <title>A General Quadratic Application</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Khan Noonien Singh has set the stolen Genesis device to detonate to destroy the Enterprise, Captain Kirk and his crew. When the Genesis device charges to 30 it 
            will explode. The equation <m>c=t^2-2t+22</m> models the device charging where <m>t</m> is the time in minutes and <m>c</m> is the charge. How long does the crew
            of the Enterprise have to escape?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            <m>t = 4</m> minutes.
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            We are given a value for <m>c</m> of 30. Substitute this into the equation to get <m>30=t^2-2t+22</m>. Subtract 30 from both sides <m>0=t^2-2t-8</m>. The question
            is asking for a time (<q>How long...?</q>), so this is asking us to solve for the <m>t</m>intercepts of this transformed equation. Solve by factoring.
            <m>0=t^2-2t-8=(t-4)(t+2) \rightarrow t = -2</m> and <m>t=4</m>. Since time cannot be a negative value in this question, the solution is <m>t = 4</m> minutes.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
</page>
<page>
<example>
    <title>Projectile Motion</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
           A firecracker is fired straight up into the air out of a window of a building. Its height, in feet, is given by <m>h=-16t^2+32t+128</m>, where <m>t</m> is the time
           in seconds, the  firecracker has been in the air.
           <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
                <p>
                   How high up is the window? 
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    When does the firecracker hit the ground?
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                   What is the maximum height of the firecracker? 
                </p>
            </li>
           </ol> 
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            (a) 128 feet. (b) 4 seconds. (c) 144 feet
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Part (a) is asking about the <m>y</m> or <m>h</m>-intercept, since it is asking about the height when <m>t=0</m>. Letting <m>t=0</m>, gives <m>h=128</m> feet. Part
            (b) is asking about <m>t</m>-intercepts, since it asks <q>when does...?</q> and the ground is also where <m>h=0</m>. Setting <m>h=0</m> and factoring gives
            <m>0 = -16t^2+32t+128 = -16(t^2-2t-8) = -16(t+2)(t-4)</m> and <m>t=4</m> seconds is the only meaningful solution. Part (c) is asking about the vertex (maximum height).
            Due to the symmetry of a quadratic equation or parabola, the vertex is always at the midpoint between the <m>x</m>-intercepts. If we know the <m>x</m>-intercepts,
            we can find the <m>x</m>-value of the vertex. Here the <m>x</m> or <m>t</m> intercepts are -2 and 4. The midpoint between -2 and 4 is <m>t=1</m>. Substitute 
            <m>t=1</m> into the height equation to get a maximum height <m>h=-16(1)^2 +32(1)+128 = 144</m>.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
</page>
<page>
<example>
    <title>A Economics Application</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            A coffee shop currently sells 300 lattes a day at $3.00 each. They recently tried raising the by price by $0.25 a latte, and found that they sold 40 less lattes a day.
        </p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
            <li>
                <p>
                   Assume that the number of lattes they sell in a day, <m>N</m>, linearly related to the sale price, <m>p</m> (in dollars). Find an equation for <m>N</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Revenue (the amount of money the store brings in before costs) can be found by multiplying the cost per cup times the number of cups sold. 
                    Again using <m>p</m> as the sales price, use your equation from above to write an equation for the revenue, <m>R</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The store wants to maximize their revenue (make as much money as possible). Find the value of <m>p</m> that will maximize the revenue
                    (round to the nearest cent) and the maximum revenue.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            (a) <m>N=-160p+780</m>. (b) <m>R=-160p^2+780p</m> (c) <m>p = \$ 2.44</m> and <m>R = \$ 950.63</m>
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            (a) We are given two points about the price and the number of lattes sold. <m>(p,N) = (3, 300)</m> and <m>(p,N) = (3.25, 260)</m>. Use these points to form a
            linear equation. Slope = -160. In point-slope form, <m>N = -160(p-3)+300</m> and in slope-intercept form <m>N=-160p+780</m>. (b) Multiply the equation for the 
            number of lattes by <m>p</m> to get revenue, <m>R=-160p^2+780</m>. (c) Find the vertex fo this quadratic equation. At the vertex, <m>p=-b/2a = - 780/(-320) = 2.44</m>.
            Substitute <m>p=2.44</m> into the revenue equation to get <m>R = -160(2.44)^2+780(2.44) = 950.62</m>
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
</page>
<page>
<example>
    <title>A Geometry Problem</title>
    <statement>
        <p>
            Shannon is having a rectangular swimming pool installed in her backyard. The plan for the pool shows an area of 255 square feet and the length has to be 
            two feet more than the width. What is the length of Shannon's swimming pool?
        </p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
        <p>
            17 feet
        </p>
    </answer>
    <solution>
        <p>
            The area of a rectangle is length <m>\times</m> width. Let <m>w</m> be the width of the pool. The length is 2 feet longer than the width, so <m>l=w+2</m>. Area
            in terms of <m>w</m> is <m>255=(w)(w+2) = w^2+2w \rightarrow 0 = w^2 + 2w -255 = (w-15)(w+17)</m> gives <m>w=15</m> feet and the length is 2 feet longer or 17 feet.
        </p>
    </solution>
</example>
</page>
<page>
<p>
    <alert>Try it Now!</alert>
</p>
<exercise workspace="3.0in">
    <p>
       An alligator population in Florida's Cedar Grove State Park is given by <m>A = 2t^2+3t +27</m>, where <m>A</m> is the alligator population in hundreds and <m>t</m> is the 
       number of years since 1990. When were there 4100 alligators in the park?
    </p>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="3.0in">
    <p>
        A ball is launched vertically in the air with a velocity of 160 feet per second. Use the formula <m>h = -16t^2 + 160T</m> to determine when the ball is 384 feet from
        the ground.
    </p>
</exercise>
</page>
<page>
<exercise workspace="3.0in">
<p>
   A water balloon is launched upward at a rate of 86 feet per second. Using the formula <m>h = -16t^2 + 86t</m>, find how long it will take the balloon to reach the maximum
   height and then find the maximum height.
</p>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="3.0in">
    <p>
        The front walk from the street to Pam’s house has an area of 250 square feet. Its length is two less than four times its width. 
        Find the length and width of the sidewalk. 
    </p>
</exercise>
</page>
<page>
<exercise workspace="3.0in">
    <p>
        A Pop-Warner football punter kicks the ball at about the 6 yard line. The equation <m>h = -x^2+16x-60</m> models the flight of the ball where <m>h</m> is the height 
        of the ball and <m>x</m> is the yard line that the ball is on. At what yard line does the ball hit the ground? What is the maximum height of the ball?
    </p>
</exercise>
<exercise workspace="3.0in">
    <p>
        The number of bicycles sold <m>x</m> is related to the price <m>p</m> by the equation <m>x=2000-2p</m>. (a) Write the price as a linear equation as a function of 
        bicycles sold. (Solve for <m>p</m>.) (b) Revenue is price per item multiplied by items sold. Write the revenue as a quadratic function of <m>x</m>. Now find the (c) 
        maximum revenue, the (d) number of bicycles to achieve the maximum revenue and (e) the price at the maximum revenue.
    </p>
</exercise>
</page>
</worksheet>